{
  "concept": "A fission chain reaction becomes self-sustaining only when the fissile sample is large enough that most emitted neutrons strike other nuclei instead of escaping through the surface — the critical mass.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a): medium blue-violet filled circle representing a sub-critical sphere of fissile material",
    "Panel (b): noticeably larger blue-violet filled circle representing a critical-mass sphere",
    "Bold titles above each panel: 'Sub-critical mass' and 'Critical mass'",
    "Lower-case panel labels '(a)' and '(b)' centered beneath each circle",
    "Single pale grey-green sphere (initiating neutron) outside the upper-left of each circle",
    "Long black arrow from the initiating neutron into the first fission site in each circle",
    "Gold/yellow multi-point starburst glyphs marking individual fission events (~8 in panel a, ~20 in panel b)",
    "Black solid arrows with filled arrowheads representing emitted neutrons, radiating 2-3 per starburst",
    "Escaping neutron arrows that cross and extend past the circle boundary (majority in panel a, minority in panel b)",
    "Captured neutron arrows whose heads terminate exactly on another starburst inside the circle",
    "Neutron-fate counter/annotation per panel: 'escaped' vs 'captured (caused fission)' with fractions",
    "Color-coded legend: gold starburst = fission event, black arrow = neutron, grey sphere = initiating neutron",
    "Escape-fraction readout tied to sphere radius (surface-to-volume annotation)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "massSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "As the sphere grows, its volume (number of nuclei available for capture) rises faster than its surface area, so the fraction of neutrons that leak out drops until each fission reliably causes at least one more.",
      "label": "Mass of fissile material (relative to critical mass)",
      "range": [0.3, 1.6, 0.05],
      "default": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "panelView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Direct side-by-side comparison makes the size difference and the opposite neutron fates (mostly escaping vs mostly captured) explicit.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Sub-critical (a)", "Critical (b)", "Side by side"],
      "default": "Side by side"
    },
    {
      "id": "chainReaction",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "The chain reaction is inherently dynamic: one neutron triggers a fission that releases 2-3 neutrons, and in a critical mass each generation multiplies (1 → 2 → 4 → 8) while in a sub-critical mass the cascade dies out as neutrons leak away.",
      "label": "Fire initiating neutron / play chain reaction",
      "loop": false
    },
    {
      "id": "neutronFate",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Each individual neutron has one of two fates — escape through the surface or capture causing another fission — and the running tally of these fates is what determines the multiplication factor k.",
      "teaches_note": "hovering any arrow or starburst reveals its fate label"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Two spheres, one difference",
      "narration": "Two spheres of the same fissile material differ only in size. The small one is sub-critical; the large one is at critical mass. Watch what happens to the neutrons in each.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Side by side", "massSlider": 0.5, "chainReaction": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Neutrons leak out of a small sphere",
      "narration": "In the sub-critical sphere, a single incoming neutron starts a few fissions, but most emitted neutrons reach the surface and escape before hitting another nucleus. The cascade fizzles out.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Sub-critical (a)", "massSlider": 0.5, "chainReaction": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Neutrons stay home in a large sphere",
      "narration": "In the critical sphere, the same single neutron starts a cascade that multiplies 1 → 2 → 4 → 8, because each emitted neutron finds another nucleus before it can reach the surface. Only a few arrows escape at the edge.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Critical (b)", "massSlider": 1.0, "chainReaction": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sweep the mass through the threshold",
      "narration": "Drag the mass slider and watch the escape fraction fall as the radius grows. Around one critical mass the counters cross: neutrons captured finally equal neutrons lost, and the reaction becomes self-sustaining.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Critical (b)", "massSlider": 1.0, "chainReaction": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Beyond critical",
      "narration": "Push past the critical mass and nearly every neutron is captured; each generation is larger than the last and the reaction grows explosively.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Critical (b)", "massSlider": 1.5, "chainReaction": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Criticality is a geometry problem as much as a chemistry one: neutrons escape through the surface but are captured throughout the volume, so once the sphere is big enough that captures outnumber escapes, every fission replaces itself and the chain sustains.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two circular panels on a white background, left smaller than right, with bold black serif-free titles 'Sub-critical mass' and 'Critical mass' centered above and '(a)' / '(b)' below. Circles are flat medium blue-violet (~#8289c4) with no outline. Inside each, scatter gold-yellow many-pointed starbursts with a soft pale-yellow glow, sparse in (a) and roughly twice as many and more densely packed in (b). From each starburst draw 2-3 bold black arrows with solid triangular heads pointing outward in varied directions; in (a) most arrow tips cross and extend well beyond the circle edge, in (b) most arrow tips land precisely on another starburst and only a handful exit at the periphery. Each panel has one pale grey-green shaded sphere just outside the upper-left with a long arrow angling down-right into the first starburst. Add a small live readout under each circle, e.g. 'neutrons escaped: 11 / 14' in red and 'neutrons captured: 3 / 14' in green, updating with the mass slider. Optional faint dashed radius line with an r label to reinforce the surface-vs-volume idea. Highlight active generation during the animation by brightening the newest starbursts and drawing their arrows last."
}

